.Dd September 28, 2026
.Dt EXIT 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm exit ,
.Nm exit_group
.Nd terminate the calling thread or process
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In unistd.h
.Ft _Noreturn void
.Fn _exit "int status"
.In stdlib.h
.Ft _Noreturn void
.Fn _Exit "int status"
.Ft _Noreturn void
.Fn exit "int status"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm exit
system call terminates the calling thread, and the process with it when
the thread is its last.
.Nm exit_group
terminates every thread of the process: the others are sent
.Dv SIGKILL
and end at their next return to user mode, and the process ends with
.Fa status
whichever thread ends last.
Their i386 system call numbers are 1 and 252, respectively;
EBX contains
.Fa status .
The low eight bits of
.Fa status
are made available to the parent through
.Xr wait 2 .
When the process's threads ended through
.Nm exit
alone, the status is the one the thread whose ID is the process ID ended
with.
.Pp
Musl's
.Fn _exit
calls
.Fn _Exit ,
which issues
.Nm exit_group
and falls back to
.Nm exit
if it returns.
Musl declares no
.Fn exit_group
function.
The
.Fn exit
function declared in
.In stdlib.h
performs library termination work, including registered exit functions
and standard I/O cleanup, before calling
.Fn _Exit .
That work is not performed by the system call.
.Pp
A thread's end clears the word
.Dv CLONE_CHILD_CLEARTID
or
.Xr set_tid_address 2
named, and wakes one
.Xr futex 2
waiter on it, when another thread shares its address space, and releases
the robust futexes it holds, as
.Xr futex 2
describes.
The record of a thread that is not the process's first is freed at the
next return to user mode of any process, unless another process traces
the thread: its record then stays until its tracer's wait reports the
end, as
.Xr ptrace 2
describes.
The first thread's record stays, as a zombie no wait reports, until the
last thread ends.
.Pp
Each thread's end gives up its address space, its descriptor table and
its working directory, and the one that held the last reference to any
of them releases it then, before the end is published.
With the last thread the kernel reparents the process's children to init,
marks it as a zombie, and wakes its parent.
The scheduler then selects another process.
If none is ready, the kernel waits for interrupts and checks again.
The parent's wait reclaims the child's process record and kernel stack.
.Pp
The death of the normal system init is fatal and panics the kernel.
When the kernel debug shell is the supervisor, a transient init instead
ends and reaps every other user process before returning to that shell.
A process in a kernel operation completes the operation and releases
its locks before the terminating signal takes effect.
A process running in user mode receives the signal on return from an
interrupt.
A process already in its own end, which has given up its address space,
is sent no signal and is waited for until it has ended.
Kernel threads and the idle process remain.
The scheduler is disabled when control returns to the debug shell.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
These calls do not return to the calling program.
They report no errors.
.Sh EXAMPLES
The default suite's
.Pa tests/forktest.c
forks a child that exits with status 42.
The parent waits and checks that the child exited normally with that
status.
.Sh DIAGNOSTICS
When the debug shell is the supervisor, each exit logs:
.Dl process: exited with status STATUS
The normal init and login-shell system does not print this line;
the shell provides the command status through
.Ql $? .
.Pp
A transient init returning to the debug shell also logs
.Ql "sys_exit: init process PID exited with status STATUS, returning to kernel shell" .
.Pp
It prints the console message:
.Dl Init process exited. Returning to kernel debug shell.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr execve 2 ,
.Xr fork 2 ,
.Xr wait 2
